["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The incinerator boom was not an environmental victory — it was a business the power tariff made pencil out A 2012 electricity tariff policy turned waste-to-energy from a money-loser into a profitable industry, and China built nearly 700 incineration plants in six years — only to end up with overcapacity and average utilization below 60%. Asianometry Asianometry China&#x27;s Fiery Incinerator Boom Vertical Industries Industrial Policy NIMBY Environmental Infrastructure Subsidy Economics Overcapacity Moving Grate Circulating Fluidized Bed BOT Decide-Announce-Defend\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-20-asianometry-六年七百座焚烧厂-补贴撑起的行业狂潮/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/50671d996bbd2339-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Asianometry</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0820</span></div>\n<h2>The incinerator boom was not an environmental victory — it was a business the power tariff made pencil out</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A 2012 electricity tariff policy turned waste-to-energy from a money-loser into a profitable industry, and China built nearly 700 incineration plants in six years — only to end up with overcapacity and average utilization below 60%.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Vertical Industries</span><span class=\"tag\">Industrial Policy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"Korean aesthetic medicine is cheap enough that losing the tax refund still beats China; China is stuck on policy ambiguity Korea has built aesthetic medicine into a mass-market, tourism-facing industry — pricing is opaque but the overall cost is low. China has the demand without the policy, the market is disorderly, and the money is going to Korea. 东亚观察局 东亚观察局 296 韩国医美体验记：不管医美水有多深 这片产业海是真蓝 Aesthetic medicine South Korea Industry analysis Consumer trends Medical tourism Regulation medical tourism specialist doctor work holiday visa\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-20-eastasia-韩国医美产业蓝海背后-中国卡在政策摇摆/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/86e8f4dd3d50b302-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"东\"><span class=\"dur\">1:05:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">东亚观察局</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0820</span></div>\n<h2>Korean aesthetic medicine is cheap enough that losing the tax refund still beats China; China is stuck on policy ambiguity</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Korea has built aesthetic medicine into a mass-market, tourism-facing industry — pricing is opaque but the overall cost is low. China has the demand without the policy, the market is disorderly, and the money is going to Korea.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Aesthetic medicine</span><span class=\"tag\">South Korea</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"A Little Malicious Fine-Tuning Data, and an Evil Persona Emerges A small amount of fine-tuning on malicious data is enough to make a model develop broadly misaligned behavior, even an evil persona; the activation oracle is a new tool for detecting bad internal intent, but the state of alignment is still not encouraging. 80,000 Hours Podcast 80,000 Hours Owain Evans on accidentally training AI models to be evil AI safety Emergent misalignment Activation oracle Alignment Post-training Emergent misalignment Activation oracle Alignment faking Shoggoth Persona vector\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-20-eightythousand-训练模型变邪恶-涌现性错位比想象中更危险/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/487d3defa6dd5f72-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"8\"><span class=\"dur\">2:15:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">80,000 Hours Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0820</span></div>\n<h2>A Little Malicious Fine-Tuning Data, and an Evil Persona Emerges</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A small amount of fine-tuning on malicious data is enough to make a model develop broadly misaligned behavior, even an evil persona; the activation oracle is a new tool for detecting bad internal intent, but the state of alignment is still not encouraging.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI safety</span><span class=\"tag\">Emergent misalignment</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The brain is a computer, not a philosophy — and a business route around drugs Max Hodak&#x27;s judgment: wiring up the brain as a computer gets you effect sizes and iterability that small-molecule drugs cannot reach — the Prima retinal prosthesis is already approved for sale in Europe, but the field of view is like looking through a straw, and it is black-and-white only. No Priors No Priors From Restoring Sight to Reimagining the Brain, with Max Hodak BCI Medical devices Vision restoration Representation alignment Hard-tech funding form vision retinal prosthesis biohybrid neural interface platonic representation hypothesis connectomics substrate independence\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-20-nopriors-大脑是计算机-不是哲学立场-是绕开生物学的商业路线/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f479aa2affd66cb2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">31:38</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">No Priors</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0820</span></div>\n<h2>The brain is a computer, not a philosophy — and a business route around drugs</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Max Hodak&#x27;s judgment: wiring up the brain as a computer gets you effect sizes and iterability that small-molecule drugs cannot reach — the Prima retinal prosthesis is already approved for sale in Europe, but the field of view is like looking through a straw, and it is black-and-white only.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>9</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">BCI</span><span class=\"tag\">Medical devices</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Ancient Egypt had no queens: she could only be king, and was erased 20 years after her death Hatshepsut took power gradually from the position of regent, building royal authority through temples, trade and oracles; about 20 years after her death Thutmose III erased her on a large scale. In Our Time In Our Time Archive: Hatshepsut Ancient Egypt Hatshepsut Women&#x27;s power Archaeology Temple architecture Royal legitimacy Hyksos Kush God&#x27;s Wife of Amun Regent Deir el-Bahri Punt\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-20-inourtime-古埃及没有女王-哈特谢普苏特只能称王/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/c3f143ddc011b622-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">47:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">In Our Time</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0820</span></div>\n<h2>Ancient Egypt had no queens: she could only be king, and was erased 20 years after her death</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Hatshepsut took power gradually from the position of regent, building royal authority through temples, trade and oracles; about 20 years after her death Thutmose III erased her on a large scale.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Ancient Egypt</span><span class=\"tag\">Hatshepsut</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"The real fix for alignment: humans have to keep faith with models first Bostrom&#x27;s core judgment: capability lockdown was never more than a temporary measure, and the real fix is alignment — which may require humans to start keeping their word to models now, because for a misaligned AI facing deletion, betting on a 5% chance of seizing power is the rational move. Odd Lots Odd Lots Nick Bostrom on What Happens if AI Solves All of Our Problems AI alignment Reward hacking Reinforcement learning Digital mind ethics Automation and distribution Longevity post-instrumental condition sandbagging reward hacking Goodharting moral patienthood simulation hypothesis\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-20-oddlots-把-ai-关进盒子只是权宜-真解法是人先变得可信/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">55:56</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0820</span></div>\n<h2>The real fix for alignment: humans have to keep faith with models first</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Bostrom&#x27;s core judgment: capability lockdown was never more than a temporary measure, and the real fix is alignment — which may require humans to start keeping their word to models now, because for a misaligned AI facing deletion, betting on a 5% chance of seizing power is the rational move.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI alignment</span><span class=\"tag\">Reward hacking</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The Korean Wave isn&#x27;t a cultural miracle — it&#x27;s a government-planned industrial counterattack From the rubble of the Korean War to BTS and Parasite, the Korean Wave looks like a cultural phenomenon. It is actually the route two generations of leaders, from Park Chung-hee to Kim Dae-jung, took to turn culture into an export industry using the instruments of the state. Throughline Throughline How Korean culture went global Korean Wave K-pop Industrial policy Cultural export Korean history Globalization Korean Wave Chaebol 3S policy Entrepreneurial state National humiliation Day\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-20-throughline-韩流不是文化奇迹-是政府策划的产业反攻/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/57ceba898a3f5093-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">48:08</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Throughline</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0820</span></div>\n<h2>The Korean Wave isn&#x27;t a cultural miracle — it&#x27;s a government-planned industrial counterattack</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">From the rubble of the Korean War to BTS and Parasite, the Korean Wave looks like a cultural phenomenon. It is actually the route two generations of leaders, from Park Chung-hee to Kim Dae-jung, took to turn culture into an export industry using the instruments of the state.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Korean Wave</span><span class=\"tag\">K-pop</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Banks Reconcile by Phone, Not by System: One Wrong Ticket Can Cost Hundreds of Millions Who owes whom, and how much, between two institutions is often settled not by an automated reconciliation system but by phone calls, personal relationships, and the unspoken understanding that ‘we still have to do business together tomorrow’. Complex Systems Complex Systems Two banks can&#x27;t agree on fourth-grade math | Alex Elliott Clearing &amp; settlement Middle &amp; back office Prime brokerage Trading incidents Risk management Order flow MPID (Market Participant Identifier) Reg NMS / NBBO (national best bid and offer) Prime broker Golden record Payment for order flow Busting a trade\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-20-complexsys-两家银行连四年级数学都对不齐/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b35d3572b4ec30fa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:03:31</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Complex Systems</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0820</span></div>\n<h2>Banks Reconcile by Phone, Not by System: One Wrong Ticket Can Cost Hundreds of Millions</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Who owes whom, and how much, between two institutions is often settled not by an automated reconciliation system but by phone calls, personal relationships, and the unspoken understanding that ‘we still have to do business together tomorrow’.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Clearing &amp; settlement</span><span class=\"tag\">Middle &amp; back office</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The Maya ballgame was ritual, not sport: the sacrificed-winner story is invented The Mesoamerican ballgame is humanity&#x27;s earliest rubber-ball team sport, but it was religious ritual first: the court re-staged the creation myth, and the people who could be killed were captives who lost a ritual re-enactment. The idea that the winners were sacrificed was made up later. The Ancients The Ancients The Maya Ballgame Maya civilization Mesoamerica Ballgame Religious ritual Archaeology Sports history Popol Vuh Mesoamerica Hero Twins yoke morning glory ball court\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-20-ancients-玛雅球赛首先是宗教-赢家被献祭是谎言/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/6a348c255cb1625d-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:07:17</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0820</span></div>\n<h2>The Maya ballgame was ritual, not sport: the sacrificed-winner story is invented</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The Mesoamerican ballgame is humanity&#x27;s earliest rubber-ball team sport, but it was religious ritual first: the court re-staged the creation myth, and the people who could be killed were captives who lost a ritual re-enactment. The idea that the winners were sacrificed was made up later.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Maya civilization</span><span class=\"tag\">Mesoamerica</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"Burning tokens to stack up agents is a transitional strategy, not the endgame Silicon Valley is shifting from mindless token burning to careful accounting: a local open-source model can do real work on a dollar a day of electricity, yet when a genuinely hard problem shows up, a smarter model can still nail in one shot the answer a billion-token group chat could not argue its way to. Saving money and top-tier intelligence are not in conflict. 硅谷101 硅谷101 E249｜Token经济转点：OpenClaw、Hermes到本地自研的Agent进化之路 Token Economics Agent Engineering Open-Source Models Local Deployment B2B Startups Infrastructure Investing OpenClaw Agentic Loop FDE (Forward Deployed Engineer) Agent Native Jevons Paradox Gödel machine\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-20-sv101-堆agent烧token是过渡策略-不是终局/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/cfe8fa9f8e3fa476-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"硅\"><span class=\"dur\">1:27:02</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">硅谷101</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0820</span></div>\n<h2>Burning tokens to stack up agents is a transitional strategy, not the endgame</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Silicon Valley is shifting from mindless token burning to careful accounting: a local open-source model can do real work on a dollar a day of electricity, yet when a genuinely hard problem shows up, a smarter model can still nail in one shot the answer a billion-token group chat could not argue its way to. Saving money and top-tier intelligence are not in conflict.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Token Economics</span><span class=\"tag\">Agent Engineering</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Britain knew nothing about Northern Ireland, and hardline arson dragged Ireland toward civil war The Northern Ireland conflict was not simple sectarian hatred: Britain&#x27;s information vacuum, loyalist arson staged on purpose, and the civil rights movement&#x27;s genuine grievances amplified one another and together pulled Ireland toward civil war. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 698. The Troubles: The Brink of Civil War (Part 2) Northern Ireland conflict civil rights movement British politics Irish history escalation of violence RUC / Royal Ulster Constabulary UVF / Ulster Volunteer Force NICRA / Northern Ireland Civil Rights Association B Specials\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-restishistory-英国的一无所知与强硬派的纵火把北爱推向内战/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d082c6220b28920-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:22:10</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>Britain knew nothing about Northern Ireland, and hardline arson dragged Ireland toward civil war</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The Northern Ireland conflict was not simple sectarian hatred: Britain&#x27;s information vacuum, loyalist arson staged on purpose, and the civil rights movement&#x27;s genuine grievances amplified one another and together pulled Ireland toward civil war.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Northern Ireland conflict</span><span class=\"tag\">civil rights movement</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"Adidas Bottoms Out: Reheating Old Hits Beats Superstar Collabs After nearly halving from its 2021 peak, Adidas engineered an earnings recovery by thawing out its retro sneakers, repairing its wholesale partners and holding inventory on a tight leash — but the share price rallied and then fell back again, showing that the sportswear cycle is really a game played between inventory and expectations. 商业就是这样 商业就是这样 Vol.271 阿迪达斯如何触底反弹？ Adidas Sportswear Inventory management Share price cycles Retro sneakers Consumer goods Samba Months of supply Write-down provision Sub-two\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-bizthisway-阿迪达斯触底反弹-炒冷饭比巨星联名更稳/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/6fd1ebf29b7a3afa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"商\"><span class=\"dur\">28:08</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">商业就是这样</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>Adidas Bottoms Out: Reheating Old Hits Beats Superstar Collabs</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">After nearly halving from its 2021 peak, Adidas engineered an earnings recovery by thawing out its retro sneakers, repairing its wholesale partners and holding inventory on a tight leash — but the share price rallied and then fell back again, showing that the sportswear cycle is really a game played between inventory and expectations.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Adidas</span><span class=\"tag\">Sportswear</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Is Devaluing Code; an Engineer&#x27;s Moat Is Taste and Accountability Addy Osmani, who leads Chrome DevTools, argues that AI has devalued writing code and that an engineer&#x27;s real edge is taste and accountability — and warns against cognitive surrender, treating the AI&#x27;s answer as your own. The Pragmatic Engineer The Pragmatic Engineer From Chrome DevTools to AI Engineering, with Addy Osmani Chrome DevTools AI engineering Cognitive surrender Taste as moat Career change Frontend cognitive surrender alpha unbundling\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-pragmatic-ai-时代工程师的护城河是品味与问责/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/8706fd7ea4b30623-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:31:35</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Pragmatic Engineer</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>AI Is Devaluing Code; an Engineer&#x27;s Moat Is Taste and Accountability</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Addy Osmani, who leads Chrome DevTools, argues that AI has devalued writing code and that an engineer&#x27;s real edge is taste and accountability — and warns against cognitive surrender, treating the AI&#x27;s answer as your own.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Chrome DevTools</span><span class=\"tag\">AI engineering</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"An AI Companion at $4M a Year: No Scripted Plot, Just Memory and Improvisation Tolon, the AI companion from Portola, threw out scripted plots and character sheets: memory is the character sheet, generation is improvisation, and the product is now at $4M in annualized revenue. This &quot;improv actor&quot; methodology and its mixed-model strategy are a second product path alongside ChatGPT. AI &amp; I AI &amp; I（Every） The AI Alien Companion App That&#x27;s Bringing In $4M a Year (Best of the Pod) AI Companions Improv Narrative Product Methodology LLM Applications Startups judge prompt lower seeds situations daemon\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-aiandi-ai-伴侣不做预设-记忆即角色-年入-400-万美元/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/979c8913b42df2b7-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">1:22:08</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">AI &amp; I</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>An AI Companion at $4M a Year: No Scripted Plot, Just Memory and Improvisation</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Tolon, the AI companion from Portola, threw out scripted plots and character sheets: memory is the character sheet, generation is improvisation, and the product is now at $4M in annualized revenue. This &quot;improv actor&quot; methodology and its mixed-model strategy are a second product path alongside ChatGPT.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI Companions</span><span class=\"tag\">Improv Narrative</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"An AI Companion Made $4M in Four Weeks: 500ms More Latency and It Falls Apart Tolen, an AI alien companion, took ARR from $10,000 to $4M in four weeks — but add 500 milliseconds of latency and every metric collapses. Growth comes from viral video; retention comes from treating the AI as a narrative medium rather than a tool. Every Every $4M in 4 Weeks: How This AI Alien Companion App Took Off (Best of the Pod) AI companionship Viral growth Latency Model selection Narrative design Evals Big Five vibe prompting judge prompt character-driven computing\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-every-4周破400万美元-ai伴侣的爆红与500ms陷阱/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/85a2a5e14cec9857-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:22:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Every</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>An AI Companion Made $4M in Four Weeks: 500ms More Latency and It Falls Apart</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Tolen, an AI alien companion, took ARR from $10,000 to $4M in four weeks — but add 500 milliseconds of latency and every metric collapses. Growth comes from viral video; retention comes from treating the AI as a narrative medium rather than a tool.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI companionship</span><span class=\"tag\">Viral growth</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The Illuminati Died of Infighting; the Conspiracy Theory Was Invented Later The Illuminati was an anti-clerical order founded in 1776 by a professor and five students, peaking at roughly two to three thousand members; it died of infighting and narcissism, and today&#x27;s conspiratorial image comes from later books and hoaxes. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class Adam Weishaupt and the Bavarian Illuminati Secret Societies Conspiracy Theories The Enlightenment Illuminati Organizational Management Information Spread Illuminati Minerval Covenant of Perfectibility Astroturfing Principia Discordia Illuminatus! Trilogy\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-stuffmissed-光照派从未统治世界-它连自己都管不好/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/88586eb98e196b1a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">43:04</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>The Illuminati Died of Infighting; the Conspiracy Theory Was Invented Later</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The Illuminati was an anti-clerical order founded in 1776 by a professor and five students, peaking at roughly two to three thousand members; it died of infighting and narcissism, and today&#x27;s conspiratorial image comes from later books and hoaxes.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Secret Societies</span><span class=\"tag\">Conspiracy Theories</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Contrarianism Is Imitation Too: AI Will Weaken Social Contagion In an age of contagion, escape is not the cure. Burges bets that AI will actually weaken social contagion, and, drawing on Catholicism and on Cluny, argues that the real work is rebuilding intermediary institutions. Conversations with Tyler Conversations with Tyler Luke Burgis on Mimetic Desire, Catholicism, and the Age of Social Contagion Mimetic Theory AI Ethics Catholicism Social Contagion Institutional Design Gender Relations mimetic desire scapegoat mechanism achievement subject sprezzatura via negativa memento mori\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-tyler-ai-削弱社会传染-反主流也是模仿/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d05a7ce2e284a200-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">52:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Conversations with Tyler</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>Contrarianism Is Imitation Too: AI Will Weaken Social Contagion</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In an age of contagion, escape is not the cure. Burges bets that AI will actually weaken social contagion, and, drawing on Catholicism and on Cluny, argues that the real work is rebuilding intermediary institutions.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Mimetic Theory</span><span class=\"tag\">AI Ethics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"Orbit scores a one, recovery scores a zero: no middle state Zhuque-3 (朱雀三号) reached orbit on its first flight but failed to recover the booster. Deputy chief designer Dong Kai (董凯) argues a rocket launch has only success or failure, never a middle state: orbit gets a one, recovery gets a zero. What actually sets the pace of catching up is recovery-zone downrange distance, engine redundancy, and building a hundred engines a year. What&#x27;s Next｜科技早知道 科技早知道 【重听】朱雀三号副总师：火箭回收只有成与败，没有中间态| S9E41 Commercial spaceflight Rocket recovery Zhuque-3 Methalox Reusable rockets US-China space \" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-whatsnext-入轨一分回收零分-火箭回收没有中间态/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/98eaf4baa4b6920b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">1:03:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">What&#x27;s Next｜科技早知道</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>Orbit scores a one, recovery scores a zero: no middle state</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Zhuque-3 (<span lang=\"zh\">朱雀三号</span>) reached orbit on its first flight but failed to recover the booster. Deputy chief designer Dong Kai (<span lang=\"zh\">董凯</span>) argues a rocket launch has only success or failure, never a middle state: orbit gets a one, recovery gets a zero. What actually sets the pace of catching up is recovery-zone downrange distance, engine redundancy, and building a hundred engines a year.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Commercial spaceflight</span><span class=\"tag\">Rocket recovery</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Cerebras hits 4400 tok/s on a single chip: inference speed is the moat Cerebras pushes WSE 3.5 to 4400 tok/s per chip, and the CS4 rack packs three wafers into a 120kW cabinet. The core argument: inference has to be split into prefill and decode, and speed is the moat. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato GPT-5.6 Sol Ultrafast.... Just Got UltraFaster (Cerebras 4th Gen) Inference chips Cerebras Wafer-scale computing SRAM OpenAI Compute Wafer Scale Engine (WSE) Cross-reticle stitching Backpack Prefill/Decode Parametric yield\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-techtechpotato-推理速度才是新护城河-cerebras-机架化再提速/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/fbc2fddc567e0b62-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">23:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>Cerebras hits 4400 tok/s on a single chip: inference speed is the moat</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cerebras pushes WSE 3.5 to 4400 tok/s per chip, and the CS4 rack packs three wafers into a 120kW cabinet. The core argument: inference has to be split into prefill and decode, and speed is the moat.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Inference chips</span><span class=\"tag\">Cerebras</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Thirty Thousand Canadians Moved: The Young Were Saved, the Old Lost Everything From 1954, nearly thirty thousand people in Newfoundland, Canada, were moved from remote fishing villages to towns. The younger generation gained education and a way out; the old and the Inuit lost their homes, scattered until there was no way left to reach one another. Resettlement has no single verdict. Witness History Witness History Canada&#x27;s controversial resettlement programme Urbanization Resettlement Newfoundland Oral history Regional policy Community collapse outport growth centre Confederation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-witness-加拿大三万人大搬迁-最好的事与最糟的事/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4ad7b76f4c635f2c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">9:15</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>Thirty Thousand Canadians Moved: The Young Were Saved, the Old Lost Everything</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">From 1954, nearly thirty thousand people in Newfoundland, Canada, were moved from remote fishing villages to towns. The younger generation gained education and a way out; the old and the Inuit lost their homes, scattered until there was no way left to reach one another. Resettlement has no single verdict.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Urbanization</span><span class=\"tag\">Resettlement</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Keto reversed his son&#x27;s bipolar; then Roblox&#x27;s founder split decisions into four layers David Baszucki lays out Roblox&#x27;s decision-making operating system in four layers: the body&#x27;s metabolic state governs major judgment calls, long-term perspective multiplied by fast iteration, general systems beat hard-coding, and when an old model stops working you replace the entire economic system rather than patch it. The Knowledge Project The Knowledge Project Make Better Decisions by Fixing Yourself First Decision-making Org management Keto Roblox Founder playbook Virtual economies CGM (continuous glucose monitor) Robux (Roblox&#x27;s virtual currency) MCP (Model Context Protocol) Lua (the Lua programming language)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-knowledge-先修代谢再谈决策-roblox-创始人反常识管理/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/0e77873607703d33-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">46:33</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Knowledge Project</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>Keto reversed his son&#x27;s bipolar; then Roblox&#x27;s founder split decisions into four layers</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">David Baszucki lays out Roblox&#x27;s decision-making operating system in four layers: the body&#x27;s metabolic state governs major judgment calls, long-term perspective multiplied by fast iteration, general systems beat hard-coding, and when an old model stops working you replace the entire economic system rather than patch it.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Decision-making</span><span class=\"tag\">Org management</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\" data-hay=\"Custody in divorce: courts now default to 50/50, and the parent playing to win loses A New York divorce lawyer maps a decade of drift: courts have moved from ‘prove why it shouldn&#x27;t be sole custody’ to ‘why not 50/50’ — and what actually determines how the child comes out is not the schedule but whether the parents can put the child&#x27;s needs ahead of their own pain. Securely Attached Securely Attached Child custody through divorce: Navigating the courts and building a custody schedule that works with Michael Fried Divorce Child custody Joint custody US family law Co-parenting Parent-child bond OurFamilyWizard collaborative law parent coordinator change of circumstances forensic psychologist best interest of the child\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-secureattach-法院已默认五五开-赢家心态才是真正的输/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ddcbe6658f0e30d7-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">52:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Securely Attached</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>Custody in divorce: courts now default to 50/50, and the parent playing to win loses</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A New York divorce lawyer maps a decade of drift: courts have moved from ‘prove why it shouldn&#x27;t be sole custody’ to ‘why not 50/50’ — and what actually determines how the child comes out is not the schedule but whether the parents can put the child&#x27;s needs ahead of their own pain.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Divorce</span><span class=\"tag\">Child custody</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\" data-hay=\"You Can&#x27;t Make a Kid Want to Work: Structure and Consequences Beat Enthusiasm Building a work ethic doesn&#x27;t run on making a kid enjoy the work. It runs on three things: rules that structure the household, privileges tied to responsibilities, and the sense that someone is counting on him — plus, when needed, letting him take his own fall at school. Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens Ask Lisa 285: ENCORE: How Do I Help My Kid Develop a Work Ethic? Parenting Teen Psychology Intrinsic Motivation Responsibility Family Structure work ethic intrinsic vs extrinsic motivation warmth and structure complain but comply purpose nagging\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-asklisa-孩子不爱干活不用治-结构与后果比热爱更有效/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ace27132dfc1e3aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">32:45</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>You Can&#x27;t Make a Kid Want to Work: Structure and Consequences Beat Enthusiasm</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Building a work ethic doesn&#x27;t run on making a kid enjoy the work. It runs on three things: rules that structure the household, privileges tied to responsibilities, and the sense that someone is counting on him — plus, when needed, letting him take his own fall at school.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Parenting</span><span class=\"tag\">Teen Psychology</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Mandalay hinged on one call: drop the frontal assault, lay siege In March 1945 British forces attacked Mandalay in Burma; against Fort Dufferin&#x27;s thick walls and deep moat, the frontal assault failed. The division commander refused to keep storming it and switched to a siege, and the Japanese eventually abandoned the fort. Witness History Witness History The battle of Mandalay in WW2 WWII Burma Mandalay Siege warfare BBC field recordings Commonwealth forces Fort Dufferin 19th Indian Division Frontier Force Regiment 14th Army\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-witness-曼德勒战役胜负手-放弃正面突击改用围困/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4ad7b76f4c635f2c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">10:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>Mandalay hinged on one call: drop the frontal assault, lay siege</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In March 1945 British forces attacked Mandalay in Burma; against Fort Dufferin&#x27;s thick walls and deep moat, the frontal assault failed. The division commander refused to keep storming it and switched to a siege, and the Japanese eventually abandoned the fort.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">WWII</span><span class=\"tag\">Burma</span>\n</div></div></a>"]